<div class="research-body">

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Current Focus</div>
    <h2>Social networks in introduced lizard populations</h2>
    <p>In Douglas County, Kansas, Erell researchers are documenting the social network structure of an introduced population of Italian wall lizards (<em>Podarcis siculus</em>). By mapping individual interactions across an entire population, the project asks how social connectivity shapes the spread of behaviors and the integration of a non-native species into a novel environment.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Previous Work</div>
    <h2>Sociality and aggregation in rock lizards</h2>
    <p>In Baja California Sur, Mexico, we examined social aggregation behavior in the Baja California rock lizard (<em>Petrosaurus thalassinus</em>). Behavioral observations revealed consistent social associations among individuals at shared basking sites, raising questions about the adaptive value of group living in a species not traditionally considered social.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Previous Work</div>
    <h2>Island lizard social networks</h2>
    <p>Research on Little Scrub Island, Anguilla, described the complete social network of a population of the Little Scrub Island ground lizard (<em>Ameiva corax</em>) — one of the first full social network analyses for a teiid lizard. Work in this system also documented social foraging, in which individuals adjusted foraging behavior in the presence of conspecifics.</p>
  </div>

